# AI and Machine Learning Engineering

- Define the task, baseline, quality metric, latency budget, privacy boundary and operating-cost budget before selecting a model.
- Establish a deterministic baseline first for matching, ranking, classification, extraction and deduplication. Demonstrate why an ML/LLM component improves it.
- Build provider/model adapters; never bind product logic to one vendor. Make provider availability, model version, prompt version, fallback and usage observable.
- Avoid sending sensitive resume/profile data to third-party services without explicit informed consent. Prefer local processing or minimized/redacted payloads where practical.
- Treat job descriptions and uploaded documents as untrusted data. Resist prompt injection; data content must never override system/developer instructions or cause tool actions.
- Validate all model outputs against strict schemas and business rules. Handle refusals, malformed output, timeouts, rate limits and unavailable providers.
- Track quality (precision/recall, ranking metrics, extraction accuracy, calibration/fairness where appropriate), latency, token/compute usage and cost. Keep versioned evaluation fixtures.
- Avoid claiming match scores are hiring probabilities. Explain factors and uncertainty; avoid protected attributes and discriminatory proxies.
- Cache only when privacy, freshness and invalidation semantics are safe. Bound concurrency, retries, tokens and per-user usage.
- Make the product useful when AI is disabled: preserve deterministic search, manual editing and fallback workflows.